  "content": {
    "id": "p16-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "By first evaluating $\\phi(100)$, find the last two digits of:",
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p16-q6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$7^{40}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "01",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}100&=2^2\\times5^2\\\\\\phi(100)&=(4-2)(25-5)\\\\\\phi(100)&=40\\end{align*}$$ Since 7 is coprime to 100, Euler’s theorem gives $7^{40}\\equiv1\\pmod{100}$. The last two digits are 01.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 32
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$8^{41}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "08\n\nApply Euler’s theorem modulo 25, since 8 is not coprime to 100, and also use divisibility by 4.",
          "worked": "$\\phi(100)=(4-2)(25-5)=40$. Euler’s theorem cannot be applied directly modulo 100 because 8 and 100 are not coprime. Since $\\phi(25)=25-5=20$ and 8 is coprime to 25, $$\\begin{align*}8^{20}&\\equiv1\\pmod{25}\\\\8^{41}&=(8^{20})^2\\times8\\\\8^{41}&\\equiv8\\pmod{25}\\end{align*}$$ Thus the possible residues modulo 100 are 8, 33, 58 and 83. Also, $8^{41}\\equiv0\\pmod4$. Only 8 is divisible by 4, so $8^{41}\\equiv8\\pmod{100}$. The last two digits are 08.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 64
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q6-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$87^{80}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "01",
          "worked": "$\\phi(100)=(4-2)(25-5)=40$. Since 87 is coprime to 100, $87^{40}\\equiv1\\pmod{100}$. $$\\begin{align*}87^{80}&=(87^{40})^2\\\\87^{80}&\\equiv1^2\\pmod{100}\\\\87^{80}&\\equiv1\\pmod{100}\\end{align*}$$ The last two digits are 01.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 36
      },
      {
        "id": "p16-q6-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$123^{321}$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "23",
          "worked": "$\\phi(100)=(4-2)(25-5)=40$. Since 123 is coprime to 100, $123^{40}\\equiv1\\pmod{100}$. $$\\begin{align*}123^{321}&=(123^{40})^8\\times123\\\\123^{321}&\\equiv1^8\\times23\\pmod{100}\\\\123^{321}&\\equiv23\\pmod{100}\\end{align*}$$ The last two digits are 23.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 40
      }
    ]
  },
